%!testlib
%!
% A Pattern colour space over every base it can be built on, against every
% operator that paints with one.
%
% PLRM 4.9 gives a Pattern space an optional underlying space: a coloured
% pattern (PaintType 1) carries its own colour and takes none, while an
% uncoloured one (PaintType 2) is painted in a colour supplied in that
% underlying space, which may be any space that is not itself a Pattern.
% So the pattern machinery meets the whole colour machinery, and the two
% multiply.
%
% WHY THIS FILE IS A CROSS PRODUCT AND NOT A SAMPLE. Both of the other
% pattern tests here paint over DeviceGray and nothing else. One base out
% of six, and the five that were never asked included the three whose
% colour is reached by running a program -- a Separation and a DeviceN
% call a tint transform, an Indexed space looks a value up -- which are
% exactly the ones that can disturb what a pattern's own machinery leaves
% behind. A sample cannot find that; only the product can, and the
% product is small enough to write out.
%
% The bases are listed once and every operator is asked over that one
% list, so a base added here is added to every case at once.


% What a procedure raised, with everything it disturbed put back.
%
% Two stacks need that, not one. A case that raises leaves the failed
% operator's operands behind, and those differ per base, so the depth is
% not predictable and a mark is what recovers it. It also leaves the
% graphics state it entered, because a raise never reaches the matching
% grestore -- so the bracket belongs here, outside the procedure, and not
% inside each case where only a case that succeeds would close it. A
% colour space left in force by a case that failed is inherited by every
% case after it, which turns one failure into a column of them and points
% every one of them at the wrong thing.

% The bases. Each entry is a procedure that BUILDS the space, and a
% procedure leaving the components a colour in that space takes.
%
% Built rather than held, because a case must not inherit what another
% case did to its fixture. A space that one case leaves altered is a
% space every later case is asking a different question about, so a
% single failure becomes a column of them and only the first is about
% what its label says. Building afresh is what makes each cell of the
% product independent, which is the whole value of writing the product
% out.
/BASES [
    { [ /DeviceGray ] }                                        { 0.5 }
    { [ /DeviceRGB ] }                                         { 0.2 0.4 0.6 }
    { [ /DeviceCMYK ] }                                        { 0.1 0.2 0.3 0.0 }
    { [ /Separation /Spot /DeviceGray { 1 exch sub } ] }       { 0.5 }
    { [ /DeviceN [ /A /B /C ] /DeviceRGB { 3 1 roll pop pop dup dup } ] }
                                                               { 0.2 0.4 0.6 }
    { [ /Indexed /DeviceRGB 1 <000000ffffff> ] }               { 1 }
    { [ /CIEBasedABC << /WhitePoint [0.9505 1.0 1.089] >> ] }  { 0.2 0.4 0.6 }
] def
/BASENAMES [ (DeviceGray) (DeviceRGB) (DeviceCMYK)
             (Separation) (DeviceN) (Indexed) (CIEBasedABC) ] def

% The operators that paint with the colour in force.
/PAINTS [ { fill } { eofill } { stroke } ] def
/PAINTNAMES [ (fill) (eofill) (stroke) ] def

/P1 << /PatternType 1 /PaintType 1 /TilingType 1 /BBox [0 0 10 10]
       /XStep 10 /YStep 10
       /PaintProc { pop 0 0 0.5 setrgbcolor 0 0 5 5 rectfill } >>
     matrix makepattern def
/P2 << /PatternType 1 /PaintType 2 /TilingType 1 /BBox [0 0 10 10]
       /XStep 10 /YStep 10
       /PaintProc { pop 0 0 5 5 rectfill } >>
     matrix makepattern def

/box { newpath 20 20 moveto 60 0 rlineto 0 60 rlineto closepath } bind def

/label { % (base) (paint) (what) . (string)
    /L3 exch def /L2 exch def /L1 exch def
    L1 length L2 length add L3 length add 8 add string
    dup 0 L3 putinterval
    dup L3 length ( over ) putinterval
    dup L3 length 6 add L1 putinterval
    dup L3 length 6 add L1 length add (, ) putinterval
    dup L3 length 8 add L1 length add L2 putinterval
} bind def

% --- uncoloured: the base is used, so every base must reach the page ---
0 1 BASENAMES length 1 sub {
    /bi exch def
    /mkspace BASES bi 2 mul get def
    /comps BASES bi 2 mul 1 add get def
    0 1 PAINTNAMES length 1 sub {
        /pi exch def
        { [ /Pattern mkspace exec ] setcolorspace
          comps exec P2 setpattern
          box PAINTS pi get exec } outcome /ok eq
        BASENAMES bi get PAINTNAMES pi get (uncoloured pattern) label assert
    } for
} for

% --- coloured: no base is involved, so this is the control that says a
%     failure above is about the base and not about patterns at large ---
0 1 PAINTNAMES length 1 sub {
    /pi exch def
    { [ /Pattern ] setcolorspace
      P1 setpattern
      box PAINTS pi get exec } outcome /ok eq
    (none) PAINTNAMES pi get (coloured pattern) label assert
} for

% --- and the spaces themselves still work without a pattern over them,
%     so a failure above cannot be blamed on the base being unsupported ---
0 1 BASENAMES length 1 sub {
    /bi exch def
    { BASES bi 2 mul get exec setcolorspace
      BASES bi 2 mul 1 add get exec setcolor
      box fill } outcome /ok eq
    BASENAMES bi get (fill) (plain colour) label assert
} for

% --- what makepattern requires, which PLRM 4.9.2 states as a table ---
% A PatternType 1 dictionary must carry PaintType, TilingType, BBox,
% XStep, YStep and PaintProc. A requirement enforced for four of them and
% not the fifth is a rule nobody can rely on, and the one likeliest to be
% left out is the one nothing goes on to read.
/REQ [ /PaintType /TilingType /BBox /XStep /YStep /PaintProc ] def
/REQNAMES [ (PaintType) (TilingType) (BBox) (XStep) (YStep) (PaintProc) ] def

/basepat << /PatternType 1 /PaintType 1 /TilingType 1 /BBox [0 0 10 10]
            /XStep 10 /YStep 10
            /PaintProc { pop 0 0 5 5 rectfill } >> def
/lacking { % /key . dict without that key
    /lk exch def
    basepat length dict
    basepat { 2 index 3 1 roll 1 index lk eq { pop pop pop }{ put } ifelse } forall
} bind def

% An entry PLRM marks required and the dictionary does not carry is
% undefined, not out of range: there is no value to be out of the range
% of. The two are asked for separately below so that a rule which starts
% answering for both is visible here.
0 1 REQNAMES length 1 sub {
    /ri exch def
    { REQ ri get lacking matrix makepattern pop } outcome /undefined eq
    REQNAMES ri get (makepattern) (a pattern lacking) label assert
} for

% and a value present but outside the range the specification names is
% out of range, which is a different fault with a different name
{ basepat length dict basepat { 2 index 3 1 roll put } forall
  dup /TilingType 47 put matrix makepattern pop } outcome /rangecheck eq
    (TilingType 47) (makepattern) (a pattern with) label assert

% --- a shading pattern's Background ---------------------------------------
%
% PLRM Table 4.11: a Background is laid over the whole area about to be
% painted, before the shading goes on, so what the gradient does not reach
% is that colour rather than the page. It is the shading PATTERN that
% honours it -- shfill is required to ignore it (PLRM 8.2), so the same
% dictionary through the two routes is meant to differ.
%
% Painted where it can be read back rather than asserted from the outside:
% a radial shading of radius 3 in the middle of a 20x20 fill leaves most of
% the area to the Background, so the corner is the background colour and not
% the gradient's.

/BgShading << /ShadingType 3 /ColorSpace /DeviceRGB
    /Coords [10 10 0 10 10 3] /Background [0 1 0]
    /Function << /FunctionType 2 /Domain [0 1] /C0 [1 0 0] /C1 [1 0 0] /N 1 >>
  >> def

{ gsave
  << /PatternType 2 /Shading BgShading >> matrix makepattern
  /Pattern setcolorspace setcolor
  0 0 20 20 rectfill
  grestore } (a shading pattern with a Background paints) works

{ gsave BgShading shfill grestore }
  (shfill takes the same dictionary and ignores the Background) works

showpage

verdict
